    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0657debc_763cbcb2",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-04-16T02:33:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks. Some thoughts (which doesn\u0027t mean that I want these in this CL)\n\n1) we might be able to merge compservice and the worker to the same binary. \n\n2) I wish we can pass the executable to run via authfs as well\n\n3) I wish we can also pass paths in addition to fds.",
      "parentUuid": "c0959b3a_766bb99f",
      "revId": "5f4379aeed06d511b606f01561435410d769021e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "edb4bb77_beef5fe8",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042276
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-04-16T17:31:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Jiyong, I feel you can read me mind ;)\n\n\u003e 1) we might be able to merge compservice and the worker to the same binary. \n\nI thought about it only briefly but didn\u0027t see a clear benefit.  We\u0027d need something like --mode\u003d{service,worker} and check/switch immediately in main, then run the current code.  Do you see other benefits?\n\n\u003e 2) I wish we can pass the executable to run via authfs as well\n\nIt\u0027s technically possible to merge the responsibility of the compsvc_worker into authfs, but what\u0027s an ideal architecture would depend again on what we want to achieve.  For this change, I tried to contain authfs and the executable inside the managing compsvc_worker, which creates namespaces, waits pid, will apply seccomp policies in the future.\n\n\u003e 3) I wish we can also pass paths in addition to fds.\n\nYup, I don\u0027t see why not for other use cases.  pvm_exec currently only takes fds only because that\u0027s what happens during dexopt (limited by existing SELinux policy, and we don\u0027t want to change that).",
      "parentUuid": "0657debc_763cbcb2",
      "revId": "5f4379aeed06d511b606f01561435410d769021e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "93a05fac_00f48d80",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-04-17T05:34:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The main motivation for (1) is size \u0026 simplicity. By merging the two binaries into one, we can save space. Also, we don\u0027t need to pass the configurations via cmdline parameter.\n\nFor (2), we have another thread with more context. Let\u0027s discuss there. Note: I am not insisting that it should be supported in this initial CL.\n\n+1 for (3).",
      "parentUuid": "edb4bb77_beef5fe8",
      "revId": "5f4379aeed06d511b606f01561435410d769021e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "55af01dd_2a79fb24",
        "filename": "compos/aidl/com/android/virt/compos/ICompService.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 28,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-04-16T01:05:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why? In compos use case, it may make sense, but in general we would like to run an executable from an apk in the VM.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 28,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 28,
        "endChar": 81
      },
      "revId": "5f4379aeed06d511b606f01561435410d769021e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fc85877c_40f2aa19",
        "filename": "compos/aidl/com/android/virt/compos/ICompService.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 28,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042276
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-04-16T17:31:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I agree. This is not yet a general enough solution for running an executable from an APK.  However, I still think the service should not open to run any executable by default.  This service itself is a big attack surface, and the developer shouldn\u0027t assume the input comes from them (although I think ideally they should be able to assume so, but it seems to be a current limitation).  So we might need a way for the app developer to configure the service somehow.\n\nTaking a step back, the SDK/API requirements are not clear to me.  We could support running executable(s) and/or native binder service(s), and the design may depend on what we really want to provide.  Also, we probably haven\u0027t thought about KeyMint for apps?\n\nSome early ideas to achieve what you want:\n * Make authfs support fd to a range within a file, e.g. an uncompressed executable in an APK may just look like an executable over authfs.\n   * Or, a executable loader in the VM to run such binaries.\n * Need a config by the app developer for the service setup.\n   * Or probably better, a programming paradigm that the developer can follow.\n\nAnyway, there are a lot of fun ahead :)",
      "parentUuid": "55af01dd_2a79fb24",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 28,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 28,
        "endChar": 81
      },
      "revId": "5f4379aeed06d511b606f01561435410d769021e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b5698117_2358c636",
        "filename": "compos/aidl/com/android/virt/compos/ICompService.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 28,
      "author": {
        "id": 1380243
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-04-16T17:36:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Exposing an interface that lets a client run an arbitrary executable within the VM defeats the purpose of using the VM for isolation in the first place. The VM must not trust anything outside itself, or allow arbitrary commands to be run on behalf of a client.",
      "parentUuid": "55af01dd_2a79fb24",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 28,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 28,
        "endChar": 81
      },
      "revId": "5f4379aeed06d511b606f01561435410d769021e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c3598b2c_ec1abce6",
        "filename": "compos/aidl/com/android/virt/compos/ICompService.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 28,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-04-17T05:34:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sure. Last week\u0027s discussion was all about this. When the VM starts, the client will give the hash (or digest) of the APK(s) that are allowed in the VM. Then the runtime in VM (compsvc) will configure authfs so that any executable it shares across VMs is from one of the allowed APKs.\n\nThe story is exactly the same as APEXes. App configures a VM with the set of APEXes (name/digest) that it wants to activate in the VM. Then at runtime, apexd will mount only the APEXes that matches with the name/digest pairs. See here https://source.corp.google.com/android/packages/modules/Virtualization/microdroid/signature/README.md",
      "parentUuid": "b5698117_2358c636",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 28,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 28,
        "endChar": 81
      },
      "revId": "5f4379aeed06d511b606f01561435410d769021e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ed2d15e8_d8a828c7",
        "filename": "compos/aidl/com/android/virt/compos/ICompService.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 28,
      "author": {
        "id": 1059815
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-04-19T14:30:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Cool stuff, Victor! If I may summarize how I understand this CL (correct me if I\u0027m wrong) - this would be the executable that is loaded by microdroid from an APK (as Jiyong described) and it acts as a generic wrapper for a given executable that exposes it via RPC Binder.\n\nThat sounds compatible with our plan for packaging/spawning payloads and I do agree with writing this in a reusable manner in case others find this useful, too.\n\nWhat I don\u0027t understand is why you would give up the strongly-typed AIDL language in favour of only using it for passing command-line arguments. I would like us to encourage best practices for defining xPC interfaces, even if it means biting the bullet and writing an AIDL for dex2oat. It will save us headaches in the future.\n\nSo in my mind, we would provide the in/out FD structs to use instead of ParcelFileDescriptor, but the execute() function should be something that the user-implemented Binder server calls internally to spawn their worker. That keeps 99% of this CL intact, just leaves it up to the users to define the actual workload-specific AIDL interface and marshalling of arguments.",
      "parentUuid": "c3598b2c_ec1abce6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 28,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 28,
        "endChar": 81
      },
      "revId": "5f4379aeed06d511b606f01561435410d769021e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "98f8556d_bbc957ae",
        "filename": "compos/aidl/com/android/virt/compos/ICompService.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 28,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042276
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-04-19T22:34:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e the client will give the hash (or digest) of the APK(s) that are allowed in the VM.\n\nI think hash/digest is not enough, if we want to allow apps to access their own secret. An app VM must be tied to their APK signature.  For the same reason, for apex digest is not enough.\n\nThe rest of the problem to run an executable would just be how to tell compsvc that executable is from offset X to Y in the APK.  One idea is to tell authfs to serve the file range (X, Y) as a file.  But again, I\u0027m not sure direct command is the right programming paradigm vs a native binder service.\n\n\u003e this would be the executable that is loaded by microdroid from an APK\n\nIt\u0027s not there yet.  As cool as it sounds for app developers to run an executable in the VM, I\u0027m not sure it\u0027s the right programming model.  An alternative is for them to define their own native binder service.  Either way, we need a way to tell compsvc where to locate the executable/libraries in the APK, or even some configuration to route different request types.\n\n\u003e What I don\u0027t understand is why you would give up the strongly-typed AIDL language in favour of only using it for passing command-line arguments.\n\nDo you mean file descriptor and not other flags?  We can definitely clean up the definition.  It *might* be possible to wrap FDs with ParcelFileDescriptor (or at least create another parcelable).",
      "parentUuid": "ed2d15e8_d8a828c7",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 28,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 28,
        "endChar": 81
      },
      "revId": "5f4379aeed06d511b606f01561435410d769021e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ce4f626d_6b583267",
        "filename": "compos/aidl/com/android/virt/compos/ICompService.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 28,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-04-20T00:22:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, APK signature would be more correct. It contains digest, so I feel fine.\n\nAnd yes, we need to pass an embedded executable in an APK as a standalone file in the VM. I think this is doable as we only need to adjust the offset and size in the fd_server side and because the executables are not compressed.\n\nI am against the native binder service idea, because that will be too cumbersome for an app developer to be able to execute something in the VM, which I believe would be the most common use case. They will be re-inventing the wheel, and it isn\u0027t something we should encourage them.",
      "parentUuid": "98f8556d_bbc957ae",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 28,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 28,
        "endChar": 81
      },
      "revId": "5f4379aeed06d511b606f01561435410d769021e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
